gpui_windows: Deliver layout characters without IME

An input handler can opt into the characters the keyboard layout
produces (dead keys, AltGr, surrogate pairs) without enabling the IME:
`InputHandler::native_character_context` names the target, and the key
down is translated with ToUnicodeEx and delivered to
`handle_native_characters` while the target is still focused and current.
Before the handler is captured, the window paints the frame and input
modality a key down would, so the captured handler is the one key
dispatch uses.

The translated key never reaches TranslateMessage, so the translation
keeps the layout's dead-key state as TranslateMessage would: a dead key
delivers nothing, and the next key delivers the composed character, or
both characters when they don't compose, as in any Windows application.
The state is the thread's, shared with TranslateMessage, so a dead key
composes with the next key whichever path takes it.

Ordinary text input also changes: after an unhandled dead key, the next
text key prefers character input, so TranslateMessage completes the
character instead of a key binding taking it.

Newport needs it for Remote apps on Windows: keys go to Linux apps as key
events, and characters the layout produces go as text.

This is a second text path beside the IME path. on_prepare_key_input,
native_character_context and handle_native_characters have defaults; a
handler that keeps them uses the IME path.

Remove when upstream GPUI delivers layout characters to a non-IME target.

